= A
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Groups in Action}

= 2
{parent=2008/a}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/a/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/a/2/a}

An <invertible matrix> is exactly an ordered <basis> of its column space. The first column has $q^n-1$ choices. After $j$ <independent> columns have been chosen, their span has $q^j$ elements, so the next column has $q^n-q^j$ choices. Therefore
$$
\boxed{|\operatorname{GL}(n,q)|=\prod_{j=0}^{n-1}(q^n-q^j).}
$$

= b
{parent=2008/a/2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/a/2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/a/2/b/i}

Choose a <basis> for the first subspace of a <flag>, extend it to a <basis> for the second, and continue until obtaining a <basis> of the whole <vector space>. Do the same for the target <flag>. The unique <linear map> sending the first adapted <basis> to the second is invertible and sends every flagged subspace to its counterpart. Hence the action is **<transitive group action>[transitive]**.

= ii
{parent=2008/a/2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/a/2/b/ii}

**Yes.** Let $g$ send the first <flag> to the second and write $d=\det g\ne0$. In an adapted <basis> of the target <flag>, scale its first vector by $d^{-1}$ and fix the others. This defines a <flag>-preserving map $h$ with determinant $d^{-1}$. Then $hg$ still sends the first <flag> to the second and has determinant one. Thus $\operatorname{SL}(n,K)$ is <transitive group action>[transitive] as well.

= iii
{parent=2008/a/2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/a/2/b/iii}

Relative to the three successive blocks of sizes $1,2,2$, the stabilizer consists of the block upper triangular <matrices>
$$
\begin{pmatrix}a&B&C\\0&D&E\\0&0&F\end{pmatrix},\qquad a\in K^\times,\quad D,F\in\operatorname{GL}(2,K).
$$
The off-diagonal blocks contain $2+2+4=8$ arbitrary <field> entries. Thus its order is $(q-1)|\operatorname{GL}(2,q)|^2q^8$. By <orbit-stabilizer theorem>,
$$
\boxed{|\Omega|=\frac{\prod_{j=0}^4(q^5-q^j)}{q^8(q-1)(q^2-1)^2(q^2-q)^2}.}
$$
Equivalently, choose the line first and then a two-dimensional subspace of the four-dimensional quotient:
$$
|\Omega|=\frac{q^5-1}{q-1}\frac{(q^4-1)(q^4-q)}{(q^2-1)(q^2-q)}.
$$
This also checks the stabilizer count independently.

= B
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Introduction to Fields}

= 2
{parent=2008/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/b/2}

The <characteristic of a field>[characteristic] is the smallest positive integer $p$ with $p1_F=0$. Such an integer exists because the additive <group> is finite. If it factored as $p=ab$ with $1<a,b<p$, then $(a1_F)(b1_F)=0$ with both factors nonzero, contradicting the absence of zero divisors. Hence it is <prime>. The elements generated by $1_F$ form a subfield isomorphic to $\mathbb F_p$. The finite set $F$ is a <finite-dimensional vector space> of some <dimension> $a\ge1$ over that subfield, so $\boxed{|F|=p^a}$.

A nonzero <polynomial> of <polynomial degree> $n$ over a <field> has at most $n$ distinct roots. This follows by <induction>: a root $r$ gives a factor $X-r$, and every different root must be a root of the remaining factor by cancellation. Thus $X^n-1$ has at most $n$ roots. In the finite multiplicative <group>, the elements whose order divides $n$ are exactly those satisfying $x^n=1$. The supplied cyclicity criterion therefore proves that **the multiplicative <group> is cyclic**.

For the <prime> of the specified form write $m=2^r s$ with $s$ odd. If $s>1$, then $2^{2^r}+1$ is a proper factor of $(2^{2^r})^s+1$, impossible. Thus $m$ is a <power of two>. Since $2^m\equiv-1\pmod p$, the order of two divides $2m$ but does not divide $m$. All divisors of the <power of two> $2m$ other than $2m$ itself divide $m$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{ord}_p(2)=2m.}
$$
It generates the multiplicative <group> precisely when $2m=p-1=2^m$. For positive integers $m$ this holds exactly at $m=1,2$: from $m=3$ onward $2^m>2m$, and the inequality persists on increasing $m$. These give $\boxed{p=3\text{ or }5}$, equivalently the stated range among Fermat <primes>.

= C
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Number Theory}

= 2
{parent=2008/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/c/2}

The $d$ distinct powers $1,a,\ldots,a^{d-1}$ all satisfy $X^d=1$. The supplied root bound says they exhaust those roots. Thus every element of order $d$ has the form $a^n$. If $g=\gcd(n,d)$, the least positive $k$ with $a^{nk}=1$ is $d/g$, so
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{ord}_p(a^n)=\frac d{\gcd(n,d)}.}
$$
This proves both directions of the first assertion.

For existence of a <primitive root> one may apply the finite-<field> cyclicity criterion proved in the preceding section. There is also a direct counting proof: for each divisor $d$ of $p-1$, either no elements have order $d$, or the preceding argument shows there are exactly $\phi(d)$. <Lagrange's theorem for finite groups>[Lagrange's theorem] partitions all $p-1$ nonzero <residue> classes by such orders, while $\sum_{d\mid p-1}\phi(d)=p-1$. Every count is at most its corresponding $\phi(d)$, so equality of the totals forces equality at every divisor. In particular there are $\boxed{\phi(p-1)}$ <primitive roots>.

Put $t=g^{(p-1)/8}$. It has order eight, so $t^4=-1$ and $t^8=1$. The specified element is $k=t+t^7=t+t^{-1}$. Since $t^{-2}=-t^2$, squaring gives $\boxed{k^2=t^2+2+t^{-2}=2\pmod p}$.

= D
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Integration}

= 3
{parent=2008/d}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/d/3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/d/3/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/d/3/a/i}

The <monotone convergence> theorem states that if $f_n$ are measurable, nonnegative functions on a measure space with $f_n\le f_{n+1}$ <almost everywhere> and $f_n\uparrow f$ <almost everywhere>, then
$$
\boxed{\int f\,d\mu=\lim_{n\to\infty}\int f_n\,d\mu.}
$$
The equality is valid in $[0,\infty]$, so finiteness is not required. Changes on null sets do not affect the conclusion.

= ii
{parent=2008/d/3/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/d/3/a/ii}

**No.** Take $f_n=\mathbf1_{[n,n+1]}$ on the <real line>. At every fixed point it eventually vanishes, so $f=0$, while $\int f_n=1$ for every $n$. Thus the limit of the integrals is one and the integral of the pointwise limit is zero. Nonnegativity alone cannot replace monotonicity or an <integrable> domination hypothesis.

= b
{parent=2008/d/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/d/3/b}

Let $S_N=\sum_{k=0}^N a_k$. These are nonnegative measurable functions increasing to the extended-valued sum $S$. <Monotone convergence> gives
$$
\boxed{\int S=\sum_{k=0}^\infty\int a_k<\infty.}
$$
A <nonnegative function> with finite integral cannot be infinite on a set of positive measure. Hence $S(x)<\infty$ <almost everywhere>, which is exactly almost-everywhere convergence of the nonnegative series. One may redefine the sum arbitrarily on the exceptional <null set> to obtain an ordinary <integrable> representative.

= c
{parent=2008/d/3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/d/3/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/d/3/c/i}

Substitute $u=\log x$, giving $dx=e^u\,du$. The nonnegative integral becomes
$$
\int_0^\infty u e^{-(\alpha-1)u}\,du.
$$
For $\alpha>1$ an <integration by parts> gives $1/(\alpha-1)^2$; its boundary term vanishes because exponential decay dominates $u$. Hence it is finite, proving integrability and
$$
\boxed{\int_1^\infty x^{-\alpha}\log x\,dx=(\alpha-1)^{-2}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=2008/d/3/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/d/3/c/ii}

For $x>1$ the geometric expansion gives
$$
\frac{\log x}{x^p(x-1)}=\sum_{k=0}^\infty x^{-(p+k+1)}\log x.
$$
Every summand is nonnegative, so <monotone convergence> permits integration term by term without a prior integrability assumption. The preceding part applies with $\alpha=p+k+1>1$, yielding
$$
\boxed{\int_1^\infty\frac{\log x}{x^p(x-1)}\,dx=\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac1{(p+k)^2}.}
$$
The series is finite for every fixed $p>0$, by comparison of its tail with $\sum k^{-2}$. This also proves integrability of the original function.

= 4
{parent=2008/d}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/d/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/d/4/a}

On the product of two sigma-finite measure spaces, <Tonelli's theorem> states that a nonnegative <measurable function> has measurable iterated-integral functions and
$$
\int_{X\times Y}f\,d(\mu\times\nu)=\int_X\int_Yf\,d\nu\,d\mu=\int_Y\int_Xf\,d\mu\,d\nu,
$$
with values allowed to be infinite. Fubini's theorem states that if $f$ is measurable and $\int_{X\times Y}|f|<\infty$, then its sections are <integrable> <almost everywhere>, both iterated integrals exist as finite integrals, and the same equality holds, also for real or complex signed functions. Tonelli applied to $|f|$ can first be used to verify the absolute-integrability hypothesis of Fubini.

= b
{parent=2008/d/4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/d/4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/d/4/b/i}

For fixed $n>0$, Tonelli gives
$$
\int_0^n\int_0^\infty |\sin x|e^{-tx}\,dt\,dx=\int_0^n\frac{|\sin x|}{x}\,dx<\infty,
$$
because the quotient is <bounded> near zero. Fubini therefore permits reversing the order for this finite $x$ <interval>. Direct integration in $x$ yields
$$
\int_0^n e^{-tx}\sin x\,dx=\frac{1-e^{-nt}(t\sin n+\cos n)}{1+t^2}.
$$
Thus the desired integral equals $\pi/2$ minus the integral of the exponential remainder. Its absolute value is at most
$$
\int_0^\infty e^{-nt}(t+1)\,dt=\frac1{n^2}+\frac1n\longrightarrow0.
$$
Consequently $\boxed{\lim_{n\to\infty}\int_0^n\sin x/x\,dx=\pi/2}$. The truncation is essential: Fubini is not being applied to an absolutely <integrable function> over the whole infinite rectangle.

= ii
{parent=2008/d/4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/d/4/b/ii}

**No.** On each <interval> $[k\pi,(k+1)\pi]$, for $k\ge1$,
$$
\int_{k\pi}^{(k+1)\pi}\frac{|\sin x|}{x}\,dx\ge\frac1{(k+1)\pi}\int_{k\pi}^{(k+1)\pi}|\sin x|\,dx=\frac2{(k+1)\pi}.
$$
The resulting harmonic lower bound diverges. Therefore $\sin x/x\notin L^1(0,\infty)$. Its convergent improper integral does not make it <Lebesgue integrable>; its positive and negative parts both have infinite integral.

= E
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Topology}

= 3
{parent=2008/e}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/e/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/e/3/a}

The distance function $x\mapsto d(x,c)$ is <continuous> because the triangle inequality gives $|d(x,c)-d(y,c)|\le d(x,y)$. Hence $\overline B(c,r)=\{x:d(x,c)\le r\}$ is the <inverse image> of the closed <interval> $(-\infty,r]$, so is closed. Since it contains $B(c,r)$, it also contains the <closure> of that <open ball>:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{cl}B(c,r)\subseteq\overline B(c,r).}
$$

= b
{parent=2008/e/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/e/3/b}

The condition is $\max(|x-1/2|,|y|)\le1/2$. Therefore the ball is the **closed square**
$$
\boxed{[0,1]\times[-1/2,1/2].}
$$
Its four <vertices> are $(0,\pm1/2)$ and $(1,\pm1/2)$.

\Image[2008-supremum-balls.png]
{title=Closed supremum-norm ball and its restriction to the L-shaped metric space}

= c
{parent=2008/e/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/e/3/c}

The induced-<metric> balls are intersections with the corresponding ambient balls. On the horizontal arm the distance from the centre is $|x-1/2|$; on the vertical arm it is $\max(1/2,y)$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{B_E=((0,1)\times\{0\}),\qquad\overline B_E=([0,1]\times\{0\})\cup(\{0\}\times[0,1/2]).}
$$
The <closure> relative to $E$ of the <open ball> is $[0,1]\times\{0\}$. Every point $(0,y)$ with $y>0$ has positive distance $y$ from that horizontal arm, so none of those points is in the <closure>. In particular $(0,1/4)$ lies in the <closed ball> but not in the <closure> of the <open ball>, proving **strict inclusion**.

= d
{parent=2008/e/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/e/3/d}

Let the radius be $r>0$. One inclusion follows from (a). For the reverse, if $\|x-c\|\le r$, set $x_n=c+(1-1/n)(x-c)$ for $n\ge2$. Then $\|x_n-c\|<r$ and $x_n\to x$, so every closed-ball point is in the <closure> of the <open ball>. Hence
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{cl}B(c,r)=\overline B(c,r)\quad(r>0).}
$$
The positive-radius convention matters: if radius zero is allowed, the <open ball> is empty whereas the <closed ball> is $\{c\}$, so the asserted equality fails at that degenerate radius.

= 4
{parent=2008/e}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/e/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/e/4/a}

In the usual <real line> take $\boxed{F_n=[n,\infty)}$. Each set is nonempty and closed, and $F_{n+1}\subseteq F_n$. No real number lies in them all, because the positive integers are unbounded. Thus their intersection is empty. These sets are not <compact>.

= b
{parent=2008/e/4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/e/4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/e/4/b/i}

<Compact> subsets of a <Hausdorff space> are closed. Regard every $K_n$ as a closed subset of the <compact> set $K_1$. Any finite intersection equals the member with largest index and is therefore nonempty. The <finite intersection property> for closed subsets of a <compact> space implies
$$
\boxed{\bigcap_{n\ge1}K_n\ne\varnothing.}
$$
Equivalently, an empty intersection would give an open cover of $K_1$ by their complements, with a finite subcover contradicting nested nonemptiness.

= ii
{parent=2008/e/4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/e/4/b/ii}

Suppose no such $n_0$ exists. Nestedness then makes every $K_n\setminus U$ nonempty. These are nested <compact> sets: $X\setminus U$ is closed and $K_n$ is <compact>. By (i) their intersection is nonempty. But that intersection is $(\bigcap_nK_n)\setminus U$, contrary to the hypothesis. Thus $K_{n_0}\subseteq U$ for some $n_0$, and nestedness gives $\boxed{K_n\subseteq U\text{ for all }n\ge n_0}$ immediately.

= c
{parent=2008/e/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/e/4/c}

The inclusion $f(\bigcap_nK_n)\subseteq\bigcap_nf(K_n)$ holds for every map. For the reverse choose $y\in\bigcap_nf(K_n)$ and let $L_n=K_n\cap f^{-1}(\{y\})$. These sets are nonempty and nested. A <Hausdorff space> has closed singletons, and <continuity> makes the fibre closed, so each $L_n$ is <compact>. Part (b)(i) gives a point $x\in\bigcap_nL_n$. Then $x\in\bigcap_nK_n$ and $f(x)=y$. Hence
$$
\boxed{f\left(\bigcap_nK_n\right)=\bigcap_nf(K_n).}
$$

= F
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Multivariable Calculus}

= 2
{parent=2008/f}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/f/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/f/2/a}

A two-dimensional <embedded submanifold> is a subset which near every point can be straightened by a smooth local coordinate change in the ambient space to an open piece of the coordinate plane $\mathbb R^2\times\{0\}$. Equivalently locally it has a regular smooth parametrization of rank two, with the induced local topology.

A <tangent vector> at $x$ is the <derivative> $\gamma'(0)$ of a smooth curve in the <submanifold> with $\gamma(0)=x$. These <derivatives> form the two-dimensional <tangent space> $T_xM$. A <normal vector> is a vector <orthogonal> to every <tangent vector>. Its space is $(T_xM)^\perp$, which has $\boxed{\dim (T_xM)^\perp=1}$ in the three-dimensional ambient Euclidean space.

= b
{parent=2008/f/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/f/2/b}

Define $F(x)=x_1^2/a^2+x_2^2/b^2+x_3^2/c^2-1$. Its <gradient> is $2(x_1/a^2,x_2/b^2,x_3/c^2)$. It cannot vanish on $F^{-1}(0)$, because the only possible zero is the origin and that point has $F=-1$. The regular level-set theorem therefore makes the ellipsoid a two-dimensional <embedded submanifold>. Its <normal space> is
$$
\boxed{N_xM=\mathbb R\,(x_1/a^2,x_2/b^2,x_3/c^2),}
$$
and the <tangent space> is the <orthogonal> complement of that line.

= c
{parent=2008/f/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/f/2/c}

If a <differentiable> function has a constrained local extremum at a point of a smooth surface, its <derivative> vanishes in every tangent direction; equivalently its <gradient> is normal to the surface. For a regular equation $F=0$ this means $\nabla V=\lambda\nabla F$ for some real multiplier.

First the box is centred at the origin: holding the other coordinates of a <vertex of a polyhedron> fixed and comparing the two possible coordinates $u,v$ in the first direction gives $u^2=v^2$, so the distinct extremes are opposites; the other two directions give the same conclusion. Write its positive half-lengths as $a r,b s,c t$, with $r^2+s^2+t^2=1$. Its volume is $8abc\,rst$. A degenerate box has zero volume, so a positive <maximum> is interior in $r,s,t>0$. <Lagrange multipliers> applied to $\log(rst)$ give $1/r=2\lambda r$, and the analogous equations imply $r=s=t=1/\sqrt3$. Alternatively the arithmetic–geometric mean inequality applied to $r^2,s^2,t^2$ proves global maximality. Thus
$$
\boxed{\text{side lengths }\frac{2a}{\sqrt3},\frac{2b}{\sqrt3},\frac{2c}{\sqrt3},\qquad V_{\max}=\frac{8abc}{3\sqrt3}.}
$$

= G
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Calculus of Variations}

= 2
{parent=2008/g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/g/2}

Assume the regular Sturm–Liouville setting: real coefficients with $p>0$, $w>0$ and sufficient <continuity> and <differentiability> for the integrations below. Apply a multiplier to $J[y]-\lambda(\int_a^bwy^2-1)$. Its <first variation> in the direction $\eta$ is
$$
2[p y'\eta]_a^b-2\int_a^b\big((p y')'+qy+\lambda wy\big)\eta\,dx.
$$
Variations vanishing at both endpoints and the supplied fundamental lemma give
$$
\boxed{-(p y')'-qy=\lambda wy.}
$$
At a free endpoint the value of $\eta$ is arbitrary, so the natural condition is $p y'=0$ there. At a fixed endpoint $\eta=0$, so no such <derivative> condition is imposed; the prescribed endpoint value is imposed instead. This distinction also allows one endpoint of each type.

For <eigenfunctions> $u,v$ of distinct <eigenvalues> $\lambda,\mu$ under the same homogeneous self-adjoint endpoint conditions, multiply their equations by $v,u$ and subtract. Integration gives
$$
(\lambda-\mu)\int_a^b wuv\,dx=[p(uv'-u'v)]_a^b=0.
$$
The boundary expression vanishes for both natural conditions and homogeneous fixed-value conditions. Thus $\boxed{\int_a^b wuv=0}$ for different <eigenvalues>.

In the specified fixed-endpoint case the eigenproblem is $-y''-y=\lambda y$, $y(0)=y(1)=0$. There are no nonzero solutions when $\lambda+1\le0$; the elementary exponential or affine solutions and the two endpoint conditions prove this. For $\lambda+1>0$, the first condition <leaves of a graph>[leaves] a sine and the second requires frequency $n\pi$. The normalized stationary functions and <eigenvalues> are
$$
\boxed{y_n(x)=\pm\sqrt2\sin(n\pi x),\qquad\lambda_n=n^2\pi^2-1,\quad n\ge1.}
$$
Distinct sine modes are <orthogonal> by the <product-to-sum formula>, or by the preceding <Green second identity>. Although they need not satisfy the natural <derivative> conditions, their fixed zero values also annihilate the boundary expression, so <orthogonality> is expected.

The <Wirtinger inequality> $\int_0^1y'^2\ge\pi^2\int_0^1y^2$ gives $J[y]\ge\pi^2-1$. For completeness it follows by writing $y=v\sin\pi x$ for a smooth function supported away from the endpoints and integrating the cross term: $\int(y'^2-\pi^2y^2)=\int\sin^2(\pi x)v'^2\ge0$. Approximation extends it to the Dirichlet Sobolev class. Equality is attained by the first sine mode; the eigenproblem or equality condition gives only its scalar multiples. Hence the two normalized minimizers are $\boxed{y=\pm\sqrt2\sin\pi x}$ and the <minimum> is $\boxed{\pi^2-1=\lambda_1}$.

There is a wording distinction in the source: the two sign choices of the <minimum> are not <orthogonal> to each other, since their <inner product> is $-1$. <Orthogonality> applies to the stationary modes with different <eigenvalues>, not to both signs of the same minimizer.

= H
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Classical Mechanics}

= 2
{parent=2008/h}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/h/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/h/2/a}

Measure the axial coordinate $z$ from the <vertex>. The slice radius is $z\tan\alpha$ and its <mass> is $dm=3mz^2H^{-3}\,dz$, since cross-sectional area is proportional to $z^2$. Its axial moment is $\tfrac12(z\tan\alpha)^2dm$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{C_O=\frac{3mH^2}{10}\tan^2\alpha.}
$$
For an axis through the <vertex> perpendicular to the <cone> axis, a thin disc contributes $[z^2+\tfrac14z^2\tan^2\alpha]dm$: the second term is its diametral moment. Integration gives the two equal transverse principal moments
$$
\boxed{A_O=B_O=\frac{3mH^2}{5}\left(1+\frac{\tan^2\alpha}{4}\right).}
$$
Rotational symmetry eliminates the mixed moments and makes these the principal axes. The <centre of mass> is at $3H/4$, so the parallel-axis theorem would give $A_G=3mH^2/80+3mH^2\tan^2\alpha/20$ and $C_G=C_O$.

= b
{parent=2008/h/2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/h/2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/h/2/b/i}

The centre-of-<mass> coordinates are
$$
a=h\sin\theta\cos\phi,\qquad b=h\sin\theta\sin\phi,\qquad c=z+h\cos\theta.
$$
Differentiation and cancellation of cross terms gives
$$
\dot a^2+\dot b^2+\dot c^2=\dot z^2+h^2(\dot\theta^2+\dot\phi^2\sin^2\theta)-2h\sin\theta\,\dot z\dot\theta.
$$
Insert this into the supplied <kinetic energy> and subtract the gravitational potential $mgc$. Thus
$$
\boxed{L=\frac{A+mh^2}{2}(\dot\theta^2+\dot\phi^2\sin^2\theta)+\frac C2(\dot\psi+\dot\phi\cos\theta)^2+\frac m2(\dot z^2-2h\sin\theta\dot z\dot\theta)-mg(z+h\cos\theta).}
$$

= ii
{parent=2008/h/2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/h/2/b/ii}

The coordinate $\psi$ is cyclic, so $p_\psi=C(\dot\psi+\dot\phi\cos\theta)$ is conserved. Initially the spin is zero, hence $\boxed{\dot\psi+\dot\phi\cos\theta=0}$ throughout. The coordinate $\phi$ is also cyclic. After imposing this zero spin, its conserved momentum becomes $(A+mh^2)\dot\phi\sin^2\theta$. Initially $\theta=\pi/2$ and $\dot\phi=\omega$, so $\boxed{\dot\phi\sin^2\theta=\omega}$.

The equation for $z$ is
$$
\frac d{dt}\left[m(\dot z-h\sin\theta\dot\theta)\right]=-mg.
$$
The quantity in parentheses is the vertical velocity of the <centre of mass>. Initially that velocity and its height are both zero: the initial <rotation> is horizontal and the <vertex> starts at rest at the reference height. Twice integrating gives
$$
\boxed{z+h\cos\theta=-\frac12gt^2.}
$$
Independently, the frictionless vertical wire exerts only horizontal force. <Gravity> is therefore the only vertical external force on the body, and the <centre of mass> undergoes vertical free fall. This gives the same equation without the angular equations.

= J
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Electromagnetism}

= 2
{parent=2008/j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/j/2}

In vacuum the source-free equations are
$$
\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0,\qquad\nabla\times\mathbf E=-\mathbf B_t,\qquad\nabla\times\mathbf B=c^{-2}\mathbf E_t.
$$
Adding the divergence equations with weights $1,ic$ gives the complex divergence equation. Adding the curl equations in the same way gives $\nabla\times\mathbf W=(i/c)\mathbf W_t$. Conversely real and imaginary parts of these two complex equations recover all four real equations.

For the cosine ansatz, differentiation yields divergence $(\boldsymbol\Omega\cdot\mathbf K)\sin(\omega t-\mathbf K\cdot\mathbf x)$ and curl $-(\boldsymbol\Omega\times\mathbf K)\sin(\omega t-\mathbf K\cdot\mathbf x)$. The time <derivative> is $-\omega\boldsymbol\Omega\sin(\omega t-\mathbf K\cdot\mathbf x)$. Since that sine is not identically zero, the required conditions are precisely
$$
\boxed{\boldsymbol\Omega\cdot\mathbf K=0,\qquad\boldsymbol\Omega\times\mathbf K=(i\omega/c)\boldsymbol\Omega.}
$$
Dotting the second equation with $\boldsymbol\Omega$ gives $\boldsymbol\Omega\cdot\boldsymbol\Omega=0$ because $\omega\ne0$. This dot product is complex bilinear, not Hermitian. Taking the <cross product> again with $\mathbf K$ and using transversality gives $-|\mathbf K|^2\boldsymbol\Omega=-\omega^2\boldsymbol\Omega/c^2$. Nonzero amplitude therefore requires $\boxed{|\mathbf K|^2=\omega^2/c^2}$.

Taking real and imaginary parts yields the full conditions
$$
\boxed{\mathbf e\cdot\mathbf K=\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf K=0,\quad\mathbf K\times\mathbf e=\omega\mathbf b,\quad\mathbf b\times\mathbf K=(\omega/c^2)\mathbf e.}
$$
They imply $\mathbf e\cdot\mathbf b=0$ and $|\mathbf e|=c|\mathbf b|$, together with the dispersion relation. Conversely a nonzero transverse $\mathbf e$, this dispersion relation and $\mathbf b=\mathbf K\times\mathbf e/\omega$ satisfy all the conditions.

The physical <fields> are $\mathbf E=\mathbf e\cos(\omega t-\mathbf K\cdot\mathbf x)$ and $\mathbf B=\mathbf b\cos(\omega t-\mathbf K\cdot\mathbf x)$. Each oscillates along one fixed real direction, so **both <fields> are linearly polarized**. Complex encoding of the two <fields> here does not mean either real <field> is circularly polarized.

