{title2=Fluid Dynamics and Waves}

= 3
{parent=2008/k}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/k/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/k/3/a}

For an analytic complex potential $f$ whose singularities lie outside $|z|=a$, the <circle> theorem adds the reflected potential
$$
F(z)=f(z)+\overline{f(a^2/\bar z)}.
$$
The added term is analytic outside the <circle> wherever the reflected original is regular; in particular no new exterior singularities occur when $f$ is regular inside the <circle>. On $|z|=a$, the relation $a^2/\bar z=z$ makes $F=f+\bar f$ real. Thus its imaginary part, the stream function, is constant on the <circle> and the normal velocity vanishes. This proves the theorem, with any harmless additive constant left free. Its usual decay condition at infinity selects the surrounding flow inherited from $f$.

Write $a_1=z_1$ for the <planar potential dipole>[dipole] position to distinguish it from the <circle> radius, now one. Substitution gives
$$
\boxed{F(z)=\frac m{z-a_1}+\frac{\bar m\,z}{1-\bar a_1z},\qquad|a_1|>1.}
$$
The image singularity is at $1/\bar a_1$, strictly inside the solid <circle>. This exterior potential has the original <planar potential dipole>[dipole] and an impermeable <cylinder> boundary.

= b
{parent=2008/k/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/k/3/b}

Across a straight impermeable wall the reflected potential is obtained by conjugating the potential after reflecting its argument. For the real axis the operation is $f(z)\mapsto\overline{f(\bar z)}$; the sum is real on that axis. For the imaginary axis it is $f(z)\mapsto\overline{f(-\bar z)}$. Applying both commuting reflections to the <planar potential dipole>[dipole] produces
$$
\boxed{F(z)=\frac m{z-a}+\frac{\bar m}{z-\bar a}-\frac{\bar m}{z+\bar a}-\frac m{z+a},\qquad a=z_1.}
$$
The other three poles are outside the first quadrant. The sum is invariant under each reflected-<conjugation> operation, hence real on each boundary axis. Both walls are therefore streamlines.

= c
{parent=2008/k/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/k/3/c}

On the line $y=-x$ one has $\overline{iz}=z$. Hence the reflected term in the printed formula is $\overline{w(z)}$, and the total is real on the line. This proves that its stream function is constant there. The nested <conjugation> is important: it makes the added term analytic as a function of $z$.

For the <planar potential dipole> its image contribution is
$$
\overline{\frac m{\overline{iz}-a}}=\frac{\bar m}{iz-\bar a}=-\frac{i\bar m}{z+i\bar a}.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{F(z)=\frac m{z-a}-\frac{i\bar m}{z+i\bar a}.}
$$
The image is the reflected point $-i\bar a$, which lies in the opposite half-plane when the original <planar potential dipole>[dipole] lies strictly inside the fluid half-plane. The wall itself is a limiting boundary, not an admissible position for the singularity.

= Solution
{parent=2008/k/3}

For <wall-induced motion of a planar potential dipole>, the <complex velocity> convention is $F'(z)=v_x-iv_y$. A singularity transported by the surrounding flow therefore satisfies $\dot{\bar a}=F'_{\mathrm{reg}}(a)$, where its own term $m/(z-a)$ is removed before differentiating. The images at a given instant depend on that instantaneous position, but the spatial <derivative> treats their parameters as fixed.

For the circular wall, the <derivative> of the regular image is $\bar m/(1-\bar a z)^2$. For the quadrant, differentiate its three image terms. For the sloping wall, differentiate its single image term. This gives respectively
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\text{(a)}\quad\dot{\bar a}&=\frac{\bar m}{(1-|a|^2)^2},\\
\text{(b)}\quad\dot{\bar a}&=-\frac{\bar m}{(a-\bar a)^2}+\frac{\bar m}{(a+\bar a)^2}+\frac m{4a^2},\\
\text{(c)}\quad\dot{\bar a}&=\frac{i\bar m}{(a+i\bar a)^2}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
These are equations for $d\bar z_1/dt$, as printed. Conjugating gives equations for $dz_1/dt$ if desired; no self-induced singular velocity is included.

= 4
{parent=2008/k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/k/4}

Take source strength $Q$ to mean total two-dimensional flux, so an isolated source has potential $Q\log(z-a)/(2\pi)$. Let $a=1+i$, $b=1/\bar a=(1+i)/2$, and put $c_Q=Q/(2\pi)$. The <circle> theorem gives, up to an additive constant,
$$
w(z)=c_Q[\log(z-a)+\log(z-b)-\log z],\qquad q(z)=w'(z)=c_Q\left[\frac1{z-a}+\frac1{z-b}-\frac1z\right].
$$
The <derivative> is single valued, which is what the force integrals require. With a positively oriented contour around the <cylinder>, the force form of Blasius' theorem is
$$
F_x-iF_y=\frac{i\rho}{2}\oint_C q(z)^2\,dz.
$$
Only the poles at zero and $b$ are enclosed; the physical source at $a$ lies outside. The <residues> of $q^2/c_Q^2$ are $2(1/a+1/b)$ at zero and $2[1/(b-a)-1/b]$ at $b$. Their sum is $2[1/a+1/(b-a)]=-2/a=-(1-i)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{F_x-iF_y=\frac{\rho Q^2}{4\pi}(1-i),\qquad F_x=F_y=\frac{\rho Q^2}{4\pi}.}
$$
For the second integral, the <residue> of $zq^2/c_Q^2$ at zero is one. At $b$ it is $1+2b[1/(b-a)-1/b]=-3$. Their sum is $-2$. Using precisely the supplied complex-moment convention gives
$$
M-iN=-\frac\rho2(2\pi i)(-2c_Q^2)=\frac{i\rho Q^2}{2\pi},\qquad\boxed{M=0,\quad N=-\frac{\rho Q^2}{2\pi}.}
$$
The vanishing physical moment $M$ is also consistent with <pressure> acting normally to a <circle>, hence radially about its centre. If strength is instead defined as the coefficient of $\log(z-a)$, replace $c_Q$ by that strength throughout; stating the flux convention fixes all factors of $2\pi$.

= M
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Probability}

= 4
{parent=2008/m}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/m/4}

The moment generating function is $M_X(t)=\mathbb E(e^{tX})$ wherever that <expectation> is finite. A useful <continuity> theorem says that if the moment generating functions $M_n$ are finite on a common <open interval> containing zero and converge pointwise there to the moment generating function of a probability distribution, then the corresponding variables converge in distribution to that law. Equivalently one may assume a finite limiting function on such a neighborhood, with the usual <continuity> at zero, and obtain the uniquely determined limiting law.

For the gamma density, integrating the exponential against the density and changing variable to $(\lambda-t)x$ gives
$$
\boxed{M_X(t)=\left(\frac\lambda{\lambda-t}\right)^\alpha,\qquad t<\lambda.}
$$
For the waiting time to one head, <independence> of successive tosses gives $\Pr(Y=j)=p(1-p)^{j-1}$ for $j\ge1$. Summing the <geometric series> gives
$$
\boxed{M_Y(t)=\frac{pe^t}{1-(1-p)e^t},\qquad (1-p)e^t<1.}
$$
The waiting times between consecutive heads are <independent> copies of $Y$, so their sum $N$ has moment generating function $M_Y(t)^k$. Consequently
$$
M_{2pN}(t)=\left[\frac{pe^{2pt}}{1-(1-p)e^{2pt}}\right]^k\longrightarrow(1-2t)^{-k}.
$$
For fixed $t<1/2$, numerator and denominator have leading terms $p$ and $p(1-2t)$ as $p\downarrow0$. A fixed neighborhood of zero is inside the convergence domain for all sufficiently small $p$, so the <continuity> theorem applies. The limiting distribution is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Gamma}(\text{shape }k,\ \text{rate }1/2),}
$$
equivalently a <chi-squared distribution> with $2k$ <degrees of freedom>.

= 5
{parent=2008/m}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/m/5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/m/5/a}

Condition on the intermediate state at time $m$. The <Markov property> and time homogeneity give
$$
p_{ij}(m+n)=\sum_k\Pr(X_m=k\mid X_0=i)\Pr(X_{m+n}=j\mid X_m=k)=\sum_kp_{ik}(m)p_{kj}(n).
$$
These nonnegative sums are valid on a countable state space. Starting from $P(0)=I$, $P(1)=P$, <induction> gives $\boxed{P(n)=P^n}$. Conditioning on the initial state now yields $\boxed{\Pr(X_n=i)=(\mu P^n)_i}$. If $\mu P=\mu$, <induction> gives $\mu P^n=\mu$ for every $n$, so all one-time distributions equal the stationary initial distribution.

= b
{parent=2008/m/5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/m/5/b}

A state is recurrent if, starting there, the probability of returning at some strictly positive time is one. It is transient if that return probability is strictly less than one. Suppose $i,j$ communicate. Choose $r,s$ with $p_{ji}(r)>0$ and $p_{ij}(s)>0$. Chapman–Kolmogorov gives
$$
p_{jj}(r+n+s)\ge p_{ji}(r)p_{ii}(n)p_{ij}(s).
$$
If $i$ is recurrent, summing over $n$ and using the supplied return-series criterion makes the return series for $j$ diverge, so $j$ is recurrent. Interchanging the states proves equivalence; therefore transience is also constant on a <communicating class>.

For the nearest-neighbor walk, return in an odd number of steps is impossible. A return in $2n$ steps requires exactly $n$ steps each way, so
$$
p_{ii}(2n)=\binom{2n}{n}[p(1-p)]^n\sim\frac{[4p(1-p)]^n}{\sqrt{\pi n}}.
$$
At $p=1/2$, the return series diverges like $\sum n^{-1/2}$. If $0<p<1$ and $p\ne1/2$, then $4p(1-p)<1$, so it converges by exponential decay. At $p=0$ or one the walk never returns. Hence $\boxed{\text{recurrence occurs exactly at }p=1/2}$.

= O
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Statistics}

= 4
{parent=2008/o}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/o/4}

Assume <independent> survival times and positive rate $\lambda$. With $S=\sum_it_i$, the <likelihood> is $L(\lambda)=\lambda^n e^{-\lambda S}$ on the observed nonnegative data. The score and negative expected second <derivative> are $n/\lambda-S$ and $n/\lambda^2$. For $S>0$, which holds almost surely under the model,
$$
\boxed{\widehat\lambda=\frac nS,\qquad I_{\mathrm{full}}(\lambda)=\frac n{\lambda^2}.}
$$
The usual <independent>-sample <likelihood> asymptotics give $\sqrt n(\widehat\lambda-\lambda)\Rightarrow N(0,\lambda^2)$. Thus an approximate Wald <interval> is
$$
\boxed{\widehat\lambda\pm z_{1-\alpha/2}\frac{\widehat\lambda}{\sqrt n}.}
$$
For a positive <interval> with the same first-order justification, apply the <delta method> to $\log\widehat\lambda$, obtaining endpoints $\widehat\lambda\exp(\pm z_{1-\alpha/2}/\sqrt n)$.

A single individual survives until $T$ with probability $s=e^{-\lambda T}$. Thus $N_T\sim\operatorname{Bin}(n,s)$ and the new <likelihood> is $\binom n{n_T}e^{-\lambda Tn_T}(1-e^{-\lambda T})^{n-n_T}$. For $0<n_T<n$, its <maximum> occurs at $s=n_T/n$, giving
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\lambda=-\frac1T\log\frac{n_T}n.}
$$
If $n_T=0$, the supremum occurs as $\lambda\to\infty$ and there is no finite <maximum>. If $n_T=n$, it occurs at the boundary $\lambda=0$ of the extended parameter space, or is only a supremum when the rate is required to be strictly positive.

A binomial experiment has information $n/[s(1-s)]$ for $s$. Since $ds/d\lambda=-Ts$, parameter transformation gives
$$
\boxed{I_T(\lambda)=\frac{nT^2s}{1-s}=\frac{nT^2}{e^{\lambda T}-1}.}
$$
As quantified by <Fisher information in an exponential survival snapshot>, the ratio of this information to the <complete>-data information is $z^2/(e^z-1)$ with $z=\lambda T>0$, strictly less than one by the supplied inequality. Hence every fixed survival snapshot loses information. Under regular large-sample conditions its efficient rate estimator has larger asymptotic <variance>, and correspondingly wider uncertainty <intervals>, than the estimator based on all individual survival times. This comparison concerns information and asymptotic uncertainty, not a claim that every realized <interval> in one experiment is wider.

= 5
{parent=2008/o}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/o/5}

For a sample size $m\ge2$ define $\overline X=m^{-1}\sum_iX_i$ and $S_X^2=(m-1)^{-1}\sum_i(X_i-\overline X)^2$. The normal-sample theorem gives
$$
\boxed{\overline X\sim N(\mu_X,\sigma^2/m),\qquad\frac{(m-1)S_X^2}{\sigma^2}\sim\chi^2_{m-1},\qquad c=\frac{m-1}{\sigma^2}.}
$$
It also states that the mean and <sample variance> are <independent>. Combining (i) and (ii), and the <independence> across samples, the correctly centred pivot is
$$
\boxed{T_0=\frac{\overline X-\overline Y-(\mu_X-\mu_Y)}{S\sqrt{1/m+1/n}}\sim t_{m+n-2}.}
$$
The source reverses the mean difference inside the subtraction. Its literal statistic is generally noncentral when $\mu_X\ne\mu_Y$, rather than having a central Student distribution. Under the <null hypothesis> both differences are zero, so this misprint does not change the requested null test.

For a two-sided level-$\alpha$ test reject when $|T|>t_{m+n-2,1-\alpha/2}$. In the numerical example the pooled <variance> is $0.038$, the standard error is $\sqrt{0.038(2/5)}\approx0.12329$, and $T\approx-2.190$. There are eight <degrees of freedom> and the supplied critical value is $2.31$. Thus **do not reject equal mean cell volume at the 5% level**. This does not establish equality of the means; it states that the observed difference does not cross this test's two-sided threshold.

Age <matching> introduces a shared block factor: the two observations in a pair can be associated, and their age-dependent means also undermine the simple unblocked <independent>-samples model. A **<paired t-test>** on the within-pair drug-minus-placebo differences accounts for that design, assuming <independent> pairs and the corresponding normal-difference model. Shared age <variation> cancels in those differences; positive within-pair association reduces their <variance> and can increase <power function of a statistical test>[power], although the reduction in <degrees of freedom> means improvement is not automatic. The paired test cannot be computed from the two marginal means and <variances> alone: the within-pair differences or <covariance> are needed.

= i
{parent=2008/o/5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/o/5/i}

<Independence> makes a difference of the two normal sample means normal, with means subtracting and <variances> adding. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\overline X-\overline Y\sim N\left(\mu_X-\mu_Y,\ \sigma^2\left(\frac1m+\frac1n\right)\right).}
$$

= ii
{parent=2008/o/5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/o/5/ii}

The variables $(m-1)S_X^2/\sigma^2$ and $(n-1)S_Y^2/\sigma^2$ are <independent> chi-squared variables with $m-1$ and $n-1$ <degrees of freedom>. <Independence> follows because the two whole samples are <independent>. Their sum is chi-squared with the sum of the <degrees of freedom>, so
$$
\boxed{\frac{(m+n-2)S^2}{\sigma^2}\sim\chi^2_{m+n-2},\qquad d=\frac{m+n-2}{\sigma^2}.}
$$
Within each normal sample the <sample mean> is <independent> of its <sample variance>, and <independence> of the samples makes the pair of means jointly <independent> of the pair of <variances>. Hence this pooled chi-squared variable is <independent> of the standardized mean difference, which is the <independence> required for the Student pivot.

= P
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= 3
{parent=2008/p}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/p/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/p/3/a}

Define $\boxed{\|v\|=\sqrt{\langle v,v\rangle}}$. Positive definiteness gives nonnegativity and separation of zero, and bilinearity gives absolute homogeneity. <Cauchy sequence>[Cauchy]–Schwarz follows by minimizing the nonnegative quadratic $\|u-tv\|^2$ in real $t$. It then gives $\|u+v\|^2\le(\|u\|+\|v\|)^2$, establishing the triangle inequality. Thus this is a norm.

= b
{parent=2008/p/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/p/3/b}

Interpret the weighted function space as measurable functions modulo equality <almost everywhere>, so the integral form is a genuine <inner product>. Assume $q\in\Pi_k\subseteq V$. If the residual is <orthogonal> to $\Pi_k$, then for $p\in\Pi_k$,
$$
\|f-p\|^2=\|(f-q)+(q-p)\|^2=\|f-q\|^2+\|q-p\|^2\ge\|f-q\|^2.
$$
Thus $q$ is a <best approximation>. Conversely, if it is best, take any $r\in\Pi_k$ and vary through $q+tr$. The <polynomial>
$$
\|f-q-tr\|^2=\|f-q\|^2-2t\langle f-q,r\rangle+t^2\|r\|^2
$$
has a <minimum> at $t=0$, so its <derivative> there vanishes. Hence $\boxed{\langle f-q,r\rangle=0}$ for every $r\in\Pi_k$. The same <Pythagorean identity> proves uniqueness of the <best approximation>.

= c
{parent=2008/p/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/p/3/c}

Write $q(x)=a+bx$. <Orthogonality> of $x^2-q$ to $1,x$ gives
$$
\begin{pmatrix}\log3&2\\2&4\end{pmatrix}\binom ab=\binom4{26/3}.
$$
The entries are obtained by integrating $1/x,1,x,x^2$ over the given <interval>. Subtracting the second equation divided by two from the first gives $(\log3-1)a=-1/3$. Thus
$$
\boxed{q(x)=-\frac1{3(\log3-1)}+\left(\frac{13}6+\frac1{6(\log3-1)}\right)x.}
$$
The <Gram matrix> is positive definite, with determinant $4(\log3-1)>0$, so this <orthogonal projection> is the unique <best approximation>.

= d
{parent=2008/p/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/p/3/d}

As explained by <admissible polynomials for a singular inverse-linear weight>, there is a domain issue: the constant <polynomial> does not belong to the weighted space, because $\int_0^1x^{-1}dx=\infty$. Hence the full space $\Pi_{n-1}$ is not a subspace of $V$, and the preceding inner-product projection theorem cannot be applied to it as stated.

Nevertheless a meaningful finite-error <best approximation> does exist after restricting to admissible <polynomials>. The target $x^n$ lies in $V$ for every $n\ge1$, since its squared integrand near zero is $x^{2n-1}$. A <polynomial> $p\in\Pi_{n-1}$ has finite error exactly when $p(0)=0$: otherwise the residual tends to a nonzero constant and its weighted squared integral diverges; if $p(0)=0$, it has at least a simple zero and is <integrable> in the required norm. Thus the admissible approximation space is
$$
\boxed{\Pi_{n-1}\cap V=\operatorname{span}\{x,x^2,\ldots,x^{n-1}\}.}
$$
For $n=1$ it is $\{0\}$, giving <best approximation> zero. For $n\ge2$ it is a finite-dimensional inner-product subspace with positive definite <Gram matrix>, so it has a unique <orthogonal projection> of $x^n$. Therefore **the full <polynomial>-space formulation is not defined inside $V$, but the finite-error interpretation has a unique <best approximation>**. Declaring that no <best approximation> can exist under any interpretation would miss this admissible subspace.

= e
{parent=2008/p/3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/p/3/e}

The best-approximation characterization gives $\langle f-q,q\rangle=0$, since $q\in\Pi_k$. Consequently
$$
\|f\|^2=\|(f-q)+q\|^2=\|f-q\|^2+\|q\|^2\ge\|q\|^2.
$$
Taking nonnegative square roots proves $\boxed{\|q\|\le\|f\|}$. Thus an <orthogonal projection> cannot increase the norm.

= 4
{parent=2008/p}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/p/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/p/4/a}

A real square <QR factorization> is $A=QR$ with $Q^\mathsf TQ=I$ and $R$ upper triangular. For an <invertible matrix> it can be made unique by requiring positive diagonal entries of $R$.

If $A$ is <orthogonal>, then $R=Q^\mathsf TA$ is <orthogonal> as well. An <orthogonal> <upper triangular matrix> is diagonal with entries $\pm1$: its first column has just its first entry, which must have modulus one, and <orthogonality> then zeros the rest of the first row; repeat inductively on the lower block. Thus
$$
\boxed{R=\operatorname{diag}(\pm1,\ldots,\pm1),}
$$
and under the positive-diagonal convention $\boxed{R=I}$, $Q=A$.

= b
{parent=2008/p/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/p/4/b}

The first column is five times $(4,0,3,0)^\mathsf T/5$. Its <orthogonal> companion in the first-and-third-coordinate plane is $(3,0,-4,0)^\mathsf T/5$, and the second column has positive residual seven times that vector. The remaining coordinate directions can therefore be read off immediately rather than performing a general four-dimensional orthogonalization. One obtains
$$
\boxed{Q=\begin{pmatrix}4/5&3/5&0&0\\0&0&1&0\\3/5&-4/5&0&0\\0&0&0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad R=\begin{pmatrix}5&1&5&2\\0&7&10&-11\\0&0&9&6\\0&0&0&1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The columns of $Q$ are visibly <orthonormal>. Taking their inner products with each column of the given <matrix> gives the displayed $R$, which also verifies $QR=A$ and positive diagonal entries.

= c
{parent=2008/p/4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/p/4/c}

At step $k$, choose a real shift $\mu_k$, factor $A_k-\mu_kI=Q_kR_k$, and set
$$
A_{k+1}=R_kQ_k+\mu_kI=Q_k^\mathsf TA_kQ_k.
$$
The last equality proves <orthogonal> similarity at every step. Thus all iterates have the same <eigenvalues>; if $A_k$ is symmetric, the last expression is symmetric, proving symmetry by <induction>.

For the specified <matrix>, let $e=(1,\ldots,1)^\mathsf T$. Then $A=I-2ee^\mathsf T/n$ and $e^\mathsf Te=n$, so $A^\mathsf T=A$ and $A^2=I$. It is a <Householder reflection>, hence <orthogonal>. With the standard positive-diagonal QR convention, part (a) gives $Q=A$, $R=I$ at the first unshifted step, and therefore $A_1=RQ=A$. The same repeats forever: **the unshifted <algorithm> is stationary**.

If arbitrary diagonal signs in QR are allowed, $R=D$, $Q=AD$ for a sign <matrix> $D$, and the next iterate is $DAD$. Its off-diagonal magnitudes are unchanged, so even then only sign changes occur, not progress toward a diagonal form. This explains the role of the usual convention in the literal stationary-iterate statement.

= R
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Graph Theory}

= 2
{parent=2008/r}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/r/2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/r/2/i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/r/2/i/a}

For a finite <graph> choose a simple path of <maximum> length. Every neighbor of its final <vertex> must already lie on the path, since otherwise that <vertex> could be appended. The final <vertex> has <degree of a vertex>[degree] at least two, so besides its predecessor it has another neighbor earlier on the path. That <edge> together with the intervening path segment makes a <cycle in a graph>.

Finiteness is needed for this assertion in general: the two-sided infinite path has <degree of a vertex>[degree] two at every <vertex> and no <cycle in a graph>. The customary finite-<graph> convention is used here and in the subsequent cube questions.

= b
{parent=2008/r/2/i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/r/2/i/b}

For a closed <Eulerian trail>, every visit to a <vertex> pairs one entering <edge> with one departing <edge>, including the initial/final <vertex>. Thus every <degree of a vertex>[degree] is even.

Conversely, in a finite <connected graph> with all degrees even, start at a <vertex> and follow unused <edges> until no extension is possible. The walk cannot stop away from its starting point: at a different endpoint an odd number of its incident <edges> would have been used, leaving at least one unused <edge> because its total <degree of a vertex>[degree] is even. Thus it closes. If any <edge> remains unused, <connectedness> gives a <vertex> of the current closed trail incident with an unused <edge>. Repeat the construction from that <vertex> in the unused-<edge> subgraph and splice the resulting closed trail into the first one. Finitely many <edges> make the procedure terminate in a closed trail using every <edge> exactly once.

Hence **a <connected> finite <graph> has an Eulerian circuit exactly when all degrees are even**. If “trail” is allowed to be open, the printed equivalence needs correction: a <connected graph> has an <Eulerian trail> when it has zero or two odd-<degree of a vertex>[degree] <vertices>. A single <edge> is an immediate counterexample to the unqualified printed statement.

= ii
{parent=2008/r/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/r/2/ii}

Label <vertices> by triples of bits and join pairs differing in exactly one bit. Draw the four <vertices> with first bit zero as an outer square, and the four with first bit one as an inner square, in the same cyclic order $00,01,11,10$ for the last two bits. Connect corresponding corners by four nonintersecting spokes. Every cube <edge> is present and there are no crossings, giving the required planar embedding.

\Image[2008-planar-cube.png]
{title=Planar embedding of the cube graph with binary vertex labels}

= iii
{parent=2008/r/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/r/2/iii}

Opposite <vertices> differ in all three bits. Thus the four added <edges> are
$$
\boxed{000\!-!111,\quad001\!-!110,\quad010\!-!101,\quad011\!-!100.}
$$
They form a <perfect matching> and raise the <degree of a vertex>[degree] of every <vertex> by one.

= iv
{parent=2008/r/2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/r/2/iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/r/2/iv/a}

Every cube <vertex> has <degree of a vertex>[degree] three, so $Q_3$ is **not Eulerian**; it even has eight odd-<degree of a vertex>[degree] <vertices>, so has no open <Eulerian trail>. After the four diagonals are added every <vertex> has <degree of a vertex>[degree] four, and the <graph> remains <connected>. Thus $Q'_3$ **is Eulerian**, by the circuit criterion proved above.

= b
{parent=2008/r/2/iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/r/2/iv/b}

**Both are Hamiltonian.** A <Hamiltonian cycle> already in the cube is
$$
000,001,011,010,110,111,101,100,000.
$$
Successive triples differ in exactly one bit, and each of the eight <vertices> occurs exactly once before returning. Adding <edges> preserves this <cycle in a graph>.

= c
{parent=2008/r/2/iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/r/2/iv/c}

**Both are bipartite.** Partition the <vertices> by <parity> of the number of one bits. A cube <edge> changes one bit and an interior diagonal changes three bits, so both types join opposite <parity> classes. In fact $Q'_3$ is $K_{4,4}$: from any <vertex> its three cube neighbors and its opposite <vertex> exhaust the four <vertices> of opposite <parity>.

= d
{parent=2008/r/2/iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/r/2/iv/d}

**Yes.** Colour an even-<parity> <vertex> one colour and an odd-<parity> <vertex> the other. Every cube <edge> changes <parity>, so this is a proper two-colouring. At least two colours are needed because <edges> exist.

= e
{parent=2008/r/2/iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/r/2/iv/e}

**No.** At any cube <vertex> its three incident faces are pairwise adjacent along the three incident <edges>. They would therefore require three different colours. Equivalently the dual of the planar cube embedding contains a triangle, which obstructs a face two-colouring.

= v
{parent=2008/r/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/r/2/v}

As established above, $Q'_3\cong K_{4,4}$. Select any three <vertices> in each <parity> class; all nine <edges> between the two triples are present, giving a $K_{3,3}$ subgraph. A <planar graph> has only planar subgraphs, whereas $K_{3,3}$ is nonplanar by the permitted result. Therefore $\boxed{Q'_3\text{ is nonplanar}}$.

= S
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Simulation}

= 2
{parent=2008/s}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/s/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/s/2/a}

Assume the fixed margins are compatible, so the finite state space is nonempty. At state $X$, draw an index table $Y$ from the stipulated uniform proposal law. If $X+Y$ has any negative entry, reject and stay at $X$; otherwise accept and set the next state to $X+Y$. The margins stay fixed because the proposal has zero row and column sums.

The proposal set is invariant under $Y\mapsto-Y$. Hence proposals between two feasible distinct states are symmetric. The target density is constant, so the Metropolis–Hastings acceptance probability for a feasible proposal is one. The explicit rejection of infeasible proposals is essential: fixed margins by themselves do not guarantee nonnegative entries.

= b
{parent=2008/s/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/s/2/b}

Let $L$ be the number of index tables, including the zero table. If distinct feasible states $A,B$ differ by an index table, then $P(A,B)=1/L=P(B,A)$, using the sign-reversal <bijection> for the reverse move. If they do not differ by an index table, both probabilities are zero. Since $\pi$ is uniform,
$$
\boxed{\pi(A)P(A,B)=\pi(B)P(B,A).}
$$
For equal states <detailed balance> is tautological. Thus the chain is reversible and its <uniform distribution> is stationary.

= c
{parent=2008/s/2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/s/2/c}

A finite <irreducible Markov chain>[irreducible] <aperiodic Markov chain> converges to its unique <stationary distribution> from every initial state. Finiteness holds because nonnegative entries with fixed finite row sums are <bounded>. The given connecting-sequence property makes the chain <irreducible Markov chain>[irreducible]: each feasible step in such a sequence has positive proposal probability and is accepted. The all-zero index table occurs with probability $1/L>0$ and gives a self-loop at every state, establishing <aperiodicity>. Reversibility supplied the stationary uniform law in (b). Consequently
$$
\boxed{\Pr(X_t=x)\longrightarrow\pi(x)\quad\text{for every state }x.}
$$
The conclusion also holds when the feasible state space is a singleton.

= T
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Linear Programming}

= 2
{parent=2008/t}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2008/t/2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/t/2/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/t/2/a/i}

A <mixed strategy> for Player II is a probability vector $q\ge0$ with $\sum_jq_j=1$, specifying the random choice of a column. If Player I chooses row $i$, the expected payment is $(Aq)_i$. A mixture of rows cannot exceed the <maximum> of these row expectations, so the smallest payment Player II can guarantee is
$$
\boxed{v=\min_{q\ge0,\ \mathbf1^\mathsf Tq=1}\max_i(Aq)_i.}
$$
An optimal strategy is a vector attaining that <minimum>, which exists by <compactness> of the <probability simplex> and <continuity>. The <minimax theorem> identifies this guaranteed loss with Player I's maximal guaranteed gain, the value of the finite <zero-sum game>.

= ii
{parent=2008/t/2/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/t/2/a/ii}

Strictly positive entries imply $v>0$. If $q$ is an optimal strategy, set $x=q/v$. Then $Ax\le\mathbf1$ and $\mathbf1^\mathsf Tx=1/v$. Conversely, a nonzero feasible $x$ with $s=\mathbf1^\mathsf Tx$ gives a strategy $q=x/s$ satisfying $\max_i(Aq)_i\le1/s$. Thus $v\le1/s$, or $s\le1/v$. The optimal objective is therefore $s_*=1/v$, and
$$
\boxed{v=1/s_*,\qquad q_*=x_*/s_*.}
$$
A nonzero feasible point exists by taking a sufficiently small positive multiple of a probability vector, and positivity of $A$ bounds the feasible objective, so the optimum is attained.

For general entries choose a constant $c$ so that $A'=A+c\mathbf1\mathbf1^\mathsf T$ is strictly positive. Every pair of strategies has its payoff increased by exactly $c$, so optimal strategies are unchanged. Solve the positive problem for $A'$, recover $v'=1/s_*'$, and then $\boxed{v=v'-c}$ for the original game.

= iii
{parent=2008/t/2/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/t/2/a/iii}

The dual is
$$
\boxed{D:\ \min_{y\ge0}\mathbf1^\mathsf Ty\quad\text{subject to }A^\mathsf Ty\ge\mathbf1.}
$$
For positive payoffs and an optimal solution $y_*$, <strong duality> gives $\mathbf1^\mathsf Ty_*=1/v$. Normalizing gives Player I's optimal row strategy $p_*=y_*/(\mathbf1^\mathsf Ty_*)$. Its expected payoff against every column is at least $v$. Thus the primal represents Player II's minimization of worst loss and the dual represents Player I's maximization of guaranteed gain.

= b
{parent=2008/t/2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008/t/2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/t/2/b/i}

The proposed vectors are nonnegative, and direct multiplication gives
$$
\boxed{Ax=\binom11,\qquad A^\mathsf Ty=\begin{pmatrix}1\\5/4\\1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Thus $Ax\le\mathbf1$ and $A^\mathsf Ty\ge\mathbf1$, proving primal and <dual feasibility>.

= ii
{parent=2008/t/2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/t/2/b/ii}

Both objectives equal $1/4$: $\mathbf1^\mathsf Tx=1/12+1/6=1/4$ and $\mathbf1^\mathsf Ty=1/6+1/12=1/4$. <Weak duality> bounds the value of every primal feasible point above by every dual feasible objective. Equality for this feasible pair therefore proves **both are optimal**, without needing to solve either problem further.

= iii
{parent=2008/t/2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008/t/2/b/iii}

Normalize each optimal vector by its sum $1/4$. Player I's row strategy and Player II's column strategy are respectively
$$
\boxed{p=(2/3,1/3),\qquad q=(1/3,0,2/3),\qquad v=4.}
$$
As a direct check $Aq=(4,4)^\mathsf T$ and $p^\mathsf TA=(4,5,4)$, so Player II guarantees a loss of at most four and Player I guarantees a gain of at least four.
